## Why This Is Asked
Gauges how well a candidate designs feature-level gating in a per-user scoring API, a practical knob for privacy, performance, and experimentation.

## Key Concepts
- Feature gating: filter features at the API boundary.
- Deterministic scoring: gating should not introduce nondeterminism.
- Validation & observability: log gating decisions for audits.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: apply feature gating before scoring
function computeScoreWithGating(allFeatures, allowedFeatures){
  const mask = new Set(allowedFeatures || allFeatures.map(f => f.name));
  const included = allFeatures.filter(f => mask.has(f.name));
  return computeScoreFromFeatures(included);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to validate and handle invalid feature names?
- How would you scale gating across multiple model_versions?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-region consistency, conflict resolution, and practical MongoDB usage.

## Key Concepts
- CAP trade-offs in distributed systems
- Per-field versioning and conflict resolution
- ReadConcern/WriteConcern in MongoDB

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergePrefs(existing, incoming) {
  const out = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(existing || {}));
  for (const key of Object.keys(incoming)) {
    const inc = incoming[key];
    const cur = out[key];
    const tsInc = inc.__version?.ts ?? 0;
    const tsCur = cur?.__version?.ts ?? -1;
    if (tsInc > tsCur || (tsInc === tsCur && (inc.__version?.clientId ?? 0) < (cur?.__version?.clientId ?? 0))) {
      out[key] = inc;
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this merge under simultaneous updates?
- How would you adapt for field-level strong consistency needs?

